1
00:00:02,878 --> 00:00:07,482
Every day, we drive over them. Potholes.

2
00:00:07,934 --> 00:00:11,258
They damage our vehicles and put people in
danger.

3
00:00:11,934 --> 00:00:16,282
Maybe you reported one and never heard back.
So...

4
00:00:16,446 --> 00:00:18,554
who is actually going to fix it?

5
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:26,050
Today, we are the first group of BSIT 3H-G2. And
this

6
00:00:26,090 --> 00:00:32,230
is our promotional video for IT305, Advanced Web
Development.

7
00:00:33,098 --> 00:00:39,430
In this subject, we build web applications that
solve real community problems.

8
00:00:39,594 --> 00:00:41,606
And here is ours.

9
00:00:44,532 --> 00:00:47,088
Introducing ResQRoad:

10
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:52,112
a web and mobile-friendly pothole reporting and
monitoring system

11
00:00:52,244 --> 00:00:54,416
for local government units.

12
00:00:55,348 --> 00:01:00,432
In this system, it connects three groups on one
single platform:

13
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:06,128
residents who report the damage, administrators
who verify it,

14
00:01:06,548 --> 00:01:09,296
and road crews who fix it.

15
00:01:10,036 --> 00:01:15,792
One report. One record. From the first photo to
the verified repair.

16
00:01:17,531 --> 00:01:24,151
Too often, reporting road damage is slow and
unclear. Residents get no feedback.

17
00:01:24,699 --> 00:01:29,655
The same pothole is reported again and again and
again and again.

18
00:01:30,203 --> 00:01:33,303
And road offices lack a clear picture.

19
00:01:34,299 --> 00:01:39,479
Our group saw that gap, and we decided to design
and build

20
00:01:39,643 --> 00:01:41,943
ResQRoad to close that gap.

21
00:01:44,306 --> 00:01:46,222
Let’s start with residents.

22
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:53,454
Reporting takes four simple steps. First, give
the damage a short title.

23
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:57,422
Choose its severity and describe what you see.

24
00:01:58,546 --> 00:02:02,990
Low is a small crack, medium slows drivers down,

25
00:02:03,154 --> 00:02:06,062
and high is a deep, dangerous pothole.

26
00:02:07,506 --> 00:02:09,902
Second, set the location.

27
00:02:10,642 --> 00:02:16,302
Zoom in and place the pin right on the damage.
Third, add photos:

28
00:02:16,722 --> 00:02:21,454
a wide shot for context and a close-up of the
damage.

29
00:02:22,482 --> 00:02:24,878
Finally, review and submit.

30
00:02:25,842 --> 00:02:27,758
Your name stays private,

31
00:02:28,114 --> 00:02:32,718
and only the Road Maintenance Office sees who
filed the report.

32
00:02:33,394 --> 00:02:37,390
You get a report ID right away. And in My
Reports,

33
00:02:37,458 --> 00:02:41,582
you can follow every update from submitted to
resolved.

34
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:49,006
Before you report, open the live map. Every
reported pothole appears as a pin,

35
00:02:49,298 --> 00:02:54,894
color-coded by severity so you can see what is
already on record and avoid duplicates.

36
00:02:55,314 --> 00:03:00,046
With your report ID, Track a Report shows its
status anytime.

37
00:03:02,158 --> 00:03:06,762
Now, the LGU side. Administrators start at the
dashboard,

38
00:03:06,958 --> 00:03:12,682
which shows new reports, repairs waiting for
verification, and high-severity issues.

39
00:03:12,878 --> 00:03:19,178
In the review queue, every report opens with its
photos, location, and status timeline.

40
00:03:19,438 --> 00:03:23,338
A report is never verified just because it was
submitted.

41
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:28,714
The administrator checks the evidence, then
verifies it, rejects it,

42
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:34,378
or marks it as a duplicate. And every decision
is recorded in the report history.

43
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:41,639
Once verified, a report goes to Task Dispatch,
highest severity first.

44
00:03:41,995 --> 00:03:45,735
Administrators assign each job to a crew,

45
00:03:45,963 --> 00:03:48,807
and the workload panel keeps the work balanced.

46
00:03:49,017 --> 00:03:52,469
In the field, crews see jobs on a map,

47
00:03:52,825 --> 00:03:56,789
get directions and submit photos of the finished
repair,

48
00:03:57,273 --> 00:04:01,141
which an administrator verifies before the
report is closed.

49
00:04:03,007 --> 00:04:09,079
Behind every report is data. The Insights page
shows the resolution rate,

50
00:04:09,119 --> 00:04:14,971
average time to verify and resolve, and open
high-severity cases,

51
00:04:15,103 --> 00:04:21,435
with monthly and severity breakdowns, ready to
print or export to CSV.

52
00:04:22,914 --> 00:04:25,886
From pothole to fixed in four steps:

53
00:04:26,114 --> 00:04:29,662
Report. Verify. Repair. Resolve.

54
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,138
One transparent workflow, so residents always
know where their report stands.

55
00:04:36,526 --> 00:04:40,362
And we tested it from the first photo to the
closed report.

56
00:04:41,935 --> 00:04:47,947
ResQRoad was built because every resident
deserves safer roads,

57
00:04:48,143 --> 00:04:53,035
and every LGU deserves better tools to fix them.

58
00:04:53,519 --> 00:04:58,475
Report it. Track it. Watch your community’s
roads get fixed.

59
00:04:58,927 --> 00:05:02,667
With ResQRoad, safer roads start with one
report.
